= i
{parent=f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the field $F=\mathbb F_2[\zeta]/(\zeta^3+\zeta+1)$. The defining polynomial has no root in $\mathbb F_2$, hence is irreducible. Label its elements by
$$
1\leftrightarrow0,\quad2\leftrightarrow1,\quad3\leftrightarrow\zeta,\quad
5\leftrightarrow\zeta^2,\quad4\leftrightarrow\zeta^3,\quad
7\leftrightarrow\zeta^4,\quad8\leftrightarrow\zeta^5,\quad6\leftrightarrow\zeta^6.
$$
Since $F^*$ has prime order seven, $\zeta$ has order seven. Multiplication by $\zeta$ is exactly $b$. The identities $\zeta^3=\zeta+1$, $\zeta^6=\zeta^2+1$ and $\zeta^5=\zeta^4+1$ show that translation by one is exactly $a$.

Conjugating $a$ by powers of $b$ gives the translations $T_{\zeta^i}:z\mapsto z+\zeta^i$. The translations by $1,\zeta,\zeta^2$ generate all eight translations. Consequently
$$
\boxed{G=\{z\mapsto sz+t:s\in F^*,\ t\in F\}\cong\operatorname{AGL}_1(8),\qquad |G|=56.}
$$
For distinct $u,v$ and distinct target points $u',v'$, the unique affine map has $s=(v'-u')/(v-u)$ and $t=u'-su$. Thus the action is sharply two-transitive.

Its regular <characteristic subgroup> is
$$
\boxed{K=\{T_t:t\in F\}\cong C_2^3
=\langle a,bab^{-1},b^2ab^{-2}\rangle.}
$$
Translations act regularly, $K$ is normal, and its order eight makes it the unique Sylow two-subgroup, hence characteristic. This identifies the abstract subgroup and explicit generators in the original permutation notation.

= ii
{parent=f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Adjoin the label $9=\infty$. Inversion on the projective line, with zero and infinity interchanged, is
$$
\boxed{x=(1\ 9)(3\ 6)(5\ 8)(4\ 7).}
$$
The label $2=1\in F$ is fixed. Set $H=G_1=\{M_s:z\mapsto sz\}$, where the subscript one denotes the original point label, namely field zero. Then $x^2=1\in H$ and $xM_sx=M_{s^{-1}}$, so $x$ normalizes $H$.

For $g(z)=sz+t$ outside $H$, we have $t\ne0$. On the projective line,
$$
xgx(z)=\frac{z}{s+tz}
=\frac1t+\frac{s/t^2}{z+s/t}.
$$
The equality uses characteristic two and is valid as an equality of fractional linear transformations, including poles and infinity. Thus
$$
xgx=T_{1/t}M_{s/t^2}\,x\,T_{s/t}\in GxG.
$$
All conditions of the <double-coset criterion for a one-point extension> hold. Therefore **$G^+=\langle G,x\rangle$ is a one-point extension**, with $|G^+|=9\cdot56=504$. Its <stabilizer subgroup> is sharply two-transitive, so its action on nine points is sharply three-transitive.

= iii
{parent=f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use the projective-line realization from part (ii). The subgroup $K$ of translations is abelian and normal in the <stabilizer subgroup> $G$ of infinity. We verify both remaining conditions of <Iwasawa's simplicity lemma>, instead of concluding simplicity from transitivity alone.

Let $R$ be generated by all conjugates of $K$ in $G^+$. It contains the matrices
$$
T_t=\begin{pmatrix}1&t\\0&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
U_t=xT_tx=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\t&1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Here matrices act by fractional linear transformations. For $t\ne0$,
$$
w(t)=T_tU_{t^{-1}}T_t=\begin{pmatrix}0&t\\t^{-1}&0\end{pmatrix}.
$$
In particular $w(1)=x\in R$, and $w(t)w(1)=\operatorname{diag}(t,t^{-1})$ acts as multiplication by $t^2$. Squaring is a bijection of $F^*$, so every $M_s$ belongs to $R$. Hence $R$ contains $G$ and $x$, and $\boxed{R=G^+}$.

Choose $s\ne1$. The commutator, with convention $[u,v]=uvu^{-1}v^{-1}$, is
$$
[M_s,T_t]=T_{(s-1)t}.
$$
As $t$ varies, this gives all translations. Thus $(G^+)'$ contains $K$, and normality makes it contain every conjugate of $K$. Since those generate $G^+$, the group is a <perfect group>.

A sharply three-transitive action on nine points is a <primitive group action>, and this permutation action is a <faithful group action>. All Iwasawa hypotheses now hold, so
$$
\boxed{G^+\text{ is simple, of order }504.}
$$
It may be identified with $\operatorname{PSL}_2(8)=\operatorname{PGL}_2(8)$: the generators are fractional linear transformations and $|\operatorname{PGL}_2(8)|=8(8^2-1)=504$, while all nonzero field elements are squares. The simplicity proof above does not rely on assuming simplicity of that named family.

